<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
     The labels of the wearing diagram.

     They sit in a file separate from `strings.xml` for a reason of manufacture and not of
     tidiness: two workstreams were writing resources at the same time, and one file per
     workstream avoids the conflict. Android merges every `values/*.xml`, so the separation has no
     effect at run time — a translator will see them as a single set.

     The diagram itself carries no text (`ui/onboarding/WearingDiagram.kt`): drawn text is not
     translated. This file is therefore the speaking half of the drawing.
-->
<resources>

    <!--
         The title of the step. It says "keep identical" and not "wear it properly": the
         instruction is not that there would be a right way to wear the watch in the absolute, it
         is that the detection threshold adapts to the background noise of each night, and that two
         nights measured under different mechanical conditions are no longer comparable.
    -->
    <string name="wearing_title">Where to wear the watch</string>

    <!--
         Subtitle. "Every night the same" before the detail: it is constancy that carries the value
         of the measurement, not the choice of one side or the other.
    -->
    <string name="wearing_subtitle">The same way every night. If the mechanical conditions change, your nights stop being comparable with one another.</string>

    <!-- The three instructions the drawing shows, in the order of the drawing. -->
    <string name="wearing_point_front">On the front of the shin, just above the ankle bone.</string>

    <!--
         "Not on the bone itself" has a mechanical reason that is better given than withheld: on
         the bone, the watch turns with the foot without travelling, so it sees almost nothing.
         Without that sentence, the instruction looks like a preference and gets neglected.
    -->
    <string name="wearing_point_not_on_bone">Not on the bony bump itself: there, the watch turns with the foot without travelling, and sees almost nothing.</string>

    <!--
         This one removes a worry rather than adding a constraint. The processing chain works on
         the magnitude of the three axes, which is invariant under rotation: there is no axis to
         align, and saying so stops people trying to orient a case that does not need it.
    -->
    <string name="wearing_point_orientation">Which way the case faces does not matter: screen outwards, inwards or tilted, the reading is the same.</string>

    <!--
         What must not change from one night to the next, including what the drawing cannot show —
         the strap, its hole, the leg.
    -->
    <string name="wearing_keep_identical">Keep identical: the same leg, the same strap at the same hole, and the same spot on the shin.</string>

    <!--
         The text alternative for the diagram, for screen readers. It describes what the drawing
         **shows**, not what to do: the instructions are in the strings above, which are read out
         just afterwards. A diagram without an alternative says nothing to someone who does not see
         it, and that has already happened once in this product.
    -->
    <string name="wearing_diagram_description">Diagram. A lower leg seen from the front, with a watch drawn on the front of the shin above the ankle bones. Two crossed-out watch outlines sit on the ankle bones themselves. Below, the same watch is drawn three times with its button in a different position each time, and a curved arrow, showing that the case may be turned any way.</string>


    <!-- The button of the step. Same word as the other steps, separate entry. -->
    <string name="wearing_button">Continue</string>

</resources>
